  there will be no tourism at all and our  business really lies  on tourism  as passengers rushed to leave south  africa experts are warning that the new  variant will almost certainly reach more  countries  uw correspondent adrian kriesch is in  south africa and sent us this reaction  from cape town  the past few weeks here in cape town  almost felt too good to be true with low  infection rates life slowly got back to  normal for the first time since the  beginning of the pandemic  tourists from around the world filled up  the city once again now the new variant  was discovered and it brings back a deja  vu of sorts to what has happened about  one year ago back then south african  scientists discovered the beta variant  here and while the detection here does  not mean that it originated from south  africa  same case for the current variant by the  way it had devastating impacts on the  economy of the country there were lots  of travel restrictions imposed on south  africans who were not allowed to travel  to many countries abroad but this also  reduced the number of tourist arrivals  here in south africa a scenario that we  are likely to see again now at a time  when summer the major tourist season has  just started that would have brought  much needed boost to the tourism  industry the pandemic has made the  massive unemployment and poverty crisis  in south africa even worse and once  again no end in sight  let's bring in wolfgang prizer he's the  head of the division of medical virology  at stellenbosch university and he joins  me from cape town mr preiser welcome to  the program the who is now calling this  variant one of concern what does that  mean exactly  it means that it has properties that um  yeah that that are of of concern and  that could be that either it it  can evade the immune response it can  infect infect  vaccinated individuals or it causes more  severe disease or it is more easily  transmissible or any combination of the  above and unfortunately wh o  has come to the right conclusion this  variant at least from the genetic  properties that it has looks as if it  could do a few of those tricks  um so it's something we really need to  worry about and something we need to  investigate  very quickly  you mentioned vaccines and the potential  that this variant has to evade the  vaccines we now have this life-saving  medicine  vaccine inequality across the globe this  is a real issue would we be in this  situation if wealthier nations had done  a better job of sharing this life-saving  medicine  i speak under correction because there  are many aspects of this new variant  that we don't know yet for example where  it originated we found it here in south  africa it was also found in neighboring  botswana and then in travelers that had  come back from south africa so we think  it may have arisen in the south african  region we don't know exactly where i'm  saying that because south africa is in  the unique situation of among african  nations of actually having currently  enough vaccines for the eligible  population  the problem that we are facing is  vaccine hesitancy it's very much unlike  the industrialized countries where  there's more vaccine than there are  willing takers other african countries  of course are not in the same fortunate  position they do not have enough  vaccines so i'm not diminishing that  argument at all but should that  variant have arisen here in south africa  it could not be blamed on not having  enough vaccine it would rather have to  be blamed on  the vaccination program  population hesitancy  and resistance to to vaccination  mr preiser it was south african  researchers who detected it and they  sounded the alarm very quickly to the  rest of the world and some countries now  have placed a travel ban on that part of  the world is that the right decision do  travel bans actually work  well yes and no i i must say i have  sympathy with these decisions it seems  like the obvious thing you 