t  we're seeing is a lot of deja vu from  the first days of the pandemic around  two years ago  where investors they don't have a clear  view on things than we do but they feel  like they know where things could go  we're seeing it with the travel  restrictions and with the possibility of  lockdowns what that means is that if  there's less traveled and there's less  need for  say oil for example oil prices are  falling lockdowns would also pull back  on travel  we're also seeing that  that shares in companies that for  example are researching medicines that  are researching vaccines those are  rising and we're seeing investors try  and move their money into safer places  they're moving into things like u.s  treasuries and out of cryptocurrencies  so we're seeing a lot of movements of  investors trying to get their money into  a safer place because they're saying in  the worst case scenario this could look  like it did two years ago it's important  to remember here that many businesses  have not fully recovered from the first  wave  we're just degunking supply chains still  they're not there yet and so this could  further throw that into issue many  companies are still looking for workers  consumer behavior has changed over the  last few years so  there's a lot of complication when you  look at  you know where businesses stand as they  just start to imagine the possibility of  a new another round of a pandemic  certainly economies around the globe  have been hard hit as you mentioned some  are still struggling to recover what  kind of further setback could this new  variant now potentially have i mean it  could be pretty substantial because we  haven't seen economies fully recover  we've seen that 2022 2023 many economies  were supposed to  come closer in that direction it depends  on whether they were advanced economies  like germany like the united states  developing economies for example in  africa they were going to need longer  this could set them back even further  you know in the meantime to get there  many countries have taken on a lot of  debt and while countries like germany  and the u.s can afford that debt people  will buy it in africa they can't afford  it and also elsewhere here in the eu if  you look at the debt loads on italy for  example this could put new strains on  many countries as they try and bridge  that gap to the end of the pandemic  wherever that is or whatever that is  you know what we're also going to see  here is some fundamental questions about  globalization and the ideas behind it if  this is as bad as many people are making  it out to be that means the idea that  countries really rely on each other to  follow their strengths to trade with one  another  and to not create redundancies in the  system but if everyone says we're going  to have to close borders down we can't  rely on supply chains working then there  are big issues about  what we're going to do in the future  there's also the issue of inequality and  that's going to be a growing concern as  well many african nations again many  lower income nations we realize that  black the black economy black black uh  the black economy is really important  for these workers that tends to suffer  first when things like tourism fall and  tourism hasn't returned to many of these  countries look at bali look at south  africa they're still waiting on those  people to return and so if many of the  workers who serve them  in that economy if they're not able to  come back then that means less wages for  them that means less economic well-being  so in the end this is all about jobs and  livelihoods including what we're seeing  with wall street that's really what it  comes down to is these companies their  ability to stay on their feet and  provide jobs and livelihoods for people  dw businesses stephen beardsley thanks  for walking us through that stephen

